			"Message": "feat(Mountain): Implement enhanced IPC connection pool with health monitoring\n\nIntroduce a robust, bounded connection pool subsystem under `Source/IPC/Enhanced/ConnectionPool` to manage IPC backend resources efficiently. This module replaces ad-hoc connection handling with a scalable architecture featuring automatic lifecycle management, health scoring, and statistical telemetry.\n\nThe implementation adheres to the project's PascalCase naming and 'one struct per file' architecture:\n- `ConnectionHandle::Struct`: Tracks per-connection metadata including UUID, timestamps, error counts, and a dynamic health score that decays on failures and recovers on success.\n- `ConnectionHealth::Enum`: Defines states (`Healthy`, `Unhealthy`, `Degraded`) for precise resource filtering.\n- `HealthChecker::Struct`: Implements asynchronous health probes with configurable timeouts to validate connection viability.\n- `Pool::Struct`: The core aggregator managing a bounded semaphore, a wait queue for contention handling, and background Tokio tasks for periodic health monitoring and stale connection cleanup.\n- `PoolConfig::Struct` & `PoolStats::Struct`: Provide serialization support for tunable parameters (timeouts, bounds) and runtime telemetry (error rates, active counts).\n\nThis enhancement ensures the Mountain backend can sustain high-throughput IPC operations with automatic failover and resource recycling, crucial for the editor's stability during heavy extension host activity.",
			"SHA": "abce84b95074b8849ebd1542814a776d4541f0d5"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-03T08:17:08Z",
			"File": [
				"⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				1354,
				8
			],
			"Message": "feat(Mountain): Implement Tauri command handlers for text model lifecycle and file I/O\n\nIntroduce a comprehensive suite of Wind IPC service handlers under `Source/IPC/WindServiceHandlers/Model/` to bridge the Mountain backend with the editor's document registry and filesystem operations. These handlers fulfill the contract required by Wind's text model service to manage the full lifecycle of open files.\n\nThe implementation covers the core CRUD operations for in-memory document models and raw file access:\n- **Model Lifecycle:** `ModelOpen` reads content from disk, infers language ID from extensions, and registers a `DocumentStateDTO` in `ApplicationState.Feature.Documents` with correct EOL handling. `ModelClose` removes entries idempotently.\n- **Model State:** `ModelGet` and `ModelGetAll` return snapshots of open documents (content, version, language ID) for UI restoration and cache hydration. `ModelUpdateContent` applies edits, increments version numbers, and marks documents as dirty.\n- **File I/O:** `TextfileRead` and `TextfileWrite` provide direct verbatim file access for tooling and resolvers without polluting the document registry. `TextfileSave` acts as a save-intent hint to clear dirty markers and log traces.\n\nAll handlers adhere to PascalCase naming, strict error handling, and the project's modular architecture, ensuring the native backend can reliably synchronize state with the Wind/Sky frontend.",
			"SHA": "4f13441eff41ba8384cc274c2eca1a8d0377bac7"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-03T08:16:54Z",
			"File": [
				"⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				1345,
				5
			],
			"Message": "feat(Mountain): Implement output channel lifecycle handlers for Sky\n\nIntroduce a complete suite of Tauri command handlers under `Source/IPC/WindServiceHandlers/Output/` to manage the editor's output panel lifecycle. This bridges the Wind service layer with the Sky frontend for displaying and manipulating output streams.\n\nThe implementation adds five new modules adhering to the project's PascalCase naming and `one struct per file` architecture:\n- `OutputCreate`: Acknowledges channel creation and logs to the dev console.\n- `OutputAppend` & `OutputAppendLine`: Emit `sky://output/append` events to stream text into the Sky scroll buffer, with the latter automatically appending newlines.\n- `OutputClear` & `OutputShow`: Emit `sky://output/clear` and `sky://output/show` events respectively, allowing the frontend to reset buffers or bring a specific channel to focus.\n\nThese handlers utilize the `CommonLibrary::IPC::SkyEvent` system to trigger reactive updates in the Sky UI, completing the native backend support for the integrated output terminal.",
			"SHA": "70253806a73d36c1bcc9269c4d4c468497bf3be7"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-03T08:16:49Z",
